#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-run-workflow: the run: blocks of the steps of a workflow file, run
here as GitHub runs them, on the one system this is started on.

Usage
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow [WORKFLOW]          every job and step of the
                                                     file, by default
                                                     .github/workflows/stock-take.yml,
                                                     each run: block with the
                                                     shell the file gives it
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow --list [WORKFLOW]   the plan alone: every step
                                                     with the command its shell
                                                     makes of it and whether it
                                                     runs here; nothing is run
    scripts/devloop-run-workflow --self-test         every outcome once, in a
                                                     throwaway directory outside
                                                     the repository

Exit 0 where every step that runs here ended with 0 and no step was left
unrun but by design; 1 where a step ended otherwise than 0, was stopped at the
job's timeout, or could not be run here; 2 where the file cannot be read as a
workflow or the repository cannot be found. The last line says which: it opens
with "green:" or with "red:". Ended itself by SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP, on
macOS, Linux and Alpine, it ends the step that runs with everything it
started and exits with 130, 143 or 129 and no last line, "The end of a step"
below.

What runs, and what does not. Every step with a run: block, in the order of the
file, job by job, except three kinds, each said in the output: a step with
uses: and no run: (an action of the platform, nothing to run here); a step
whose name is one of the two the order of 31 October 2026 left out, "what the
checkout holds", which prints the runner's own tools and checkout, and "what
the container needs, under Alpine alone", which installs bash, git and python3
into the Alpine container with apk; and a step carrying an if: field, since
this program evaluates no expression of the platform - such a step is not run
and counts as red, so that a step added under a condition shows up here until
this program learns its condition, the way the workflow's own hooks step goes
red on an event it has no input for. A job with a strategy.matrix runs on
GitHub once per entry of the matrix; here it runs once, on this system, and
the output says so.

The shell, from the file and from GitHub's docs. For every step the shell is
the step's own shell: field, else the job's defaults.run.shell, else the
workflow's defaults.run.shell, else none. GitHub's page "Workflow syntax for
GitHub Actions" (docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax,
read on 31 October 2026) says under jobs.<job_id>.steps[*].shell: "You can
override the default shell settings in the runner's operating system and the
job's default using the shell keyword", and gives for each shell the command
run internally - with no shell given, on Linux and macOS, bash -e {0}, "The
default shell on non-Windows platforms. Note that this runs a different
command to when bash is specified explicitly. If bash is not found in the
path, this is treated as sh."; for bash, bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail
{0}, "The default shell on non-Windows platforms with a fallback to sh. When
specifying a bash shell on Windows, the bash shell included with Git for
Windows is used."; for sh, sh -e {0}, "The fallback behavior for non-Windows
platforms if no shell is provided and bash is not found in the path."; for
pwsh, pwsh -command ". '{0}'", "This is the default shell used on Windows";
for python, python {0}. Under "Exit codes and error action preference": "By
default, fail-fast behavior is enforced using set -e for both sh and bash",
and "When shell: bash is specified, -o pipefail is also applied to enforce
early exit from pipelines." Under jobs.<job_id>.defaults.run: "Use
jobs.<job_id>.defaults.run to provide default shell and working-directory to
all run steps in the job", and under defaults.run, "a default setting defined
in a job will override a default setting that has the same name defined in a
workflow". So a run: block of the job stock-take, which sets no shell, ends
at the first command that fails and not at a failing command inside a
pipeline, and one of the job hooks on every system, whose defaults.run.shell
is bash, ends at either, the difference pull request #205 met on 25 October
2026. {0} is the path of a file holding the block: GitHub writes the block
into a temporary file and runs the command on it; here the file lies in a
directory mktemp makes outside the repository, removed at the end. A shell
this program does not run - cmd, powershell, or one it does not know - makes
the step red with a line saying so; a custom shell, a command carrying {0}, is
run as written.

The environment. A step gets the environment this program was started in,
and on top of it the env: maps of the workflow, the job and the step, the
step's winning, with ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }} replaced by the
head of the new entry the branch adds to docs/roadmap.md - the first line
git diff origin/main...HEAD -- docs/roadmap.md adds that starts with "- **",
read in the file to its closing "**", its lines joined with one space, the
text between the markers, as the workflow's step on titles reads a head -
and empty where the diff adds none, said in the output; any other
${{ ... }} in an env: value or in a run: block leaves the step unrun and red,
since nothing here stands in for the event. Three variables the runner sets
and the steps read are set here too: GITHUB_WORKSPACE, the root of this
repository, which is also every step's working directory unless a
working-directory names another below it; RUNNER_OS, Linux, macOS or Windows
for this system, as GitHub spells them; RUNNER_TEMP, the throwaway directory
above.

The end of a step. A job's timeout-minutes stops a step that runs past it,
red, as GitHub stops the job, the minutes held against each step and not
against the job as a whole, and since 11 November 2026 the step is ended with
everything it started, so that nothing of it runs on once this program has
returned. On macOS, Linux and Alpine every step starts as the leader of a
session of its own, and at the timeout this program sends SIGKILL to the
step's process group at once and then, listing after listing of ps -A -o
pid,ppid,stat, to every process whose parent, session or process group is a
process already known as the step's, until a listing shows none of them that
is not a zombie, five seconds at most, the first listing taken before the
first signal, since a process one level down in a session of its own hangs on
the step by its parent alone; then it reads the step's output to its end. That reaches what the step's shell started and waits for, what has lost
its parent, what stands in a process group of its own, as the command and the
watchdog of bin/devloop-bounded do under the hooks step, and what was started
in a session of its own, as this program starts a step one level down in its
own self-test. It does not reach a process that left the step's session and
its parent on purpose, a daemon: no listing ties such a process to the step.
GitHub's runner ends that one too. It ends the step's one process, after
SIGINT and SIGTERM, ten seconds in all, which this program does not send,
Runner.Sdk/ProcessInvoker.cs lines 32 to 33, 443 to 465 and 855 to 869 of
actions/runner at v2.338.2, and at the job's end every process that carries
the job's RUNNER_TRACKING_ID in its environment, wherever it stands,
Runner.Worker/JobExtension.cs lines 588 to 603 and 891 to 929, the lines
"Terminate orphan process" of a job's log. The environment of another process
is not to be read on every system this program runs on: the ps of macOS 26.6
printed none under -E for a sleep of the same user, measured on 11 November
2026; the block of milestone 12 in docs/defects.md on the process that left
the step's session holds that limit, and nothing is built for it. Where ps
does not answer, the step's process group alone is ended, and where something
still stands after the five seconds, or still holds the step's output five
seconds after that, the line of the step says so behind "as GitHub stops the
job". Until that day the step ran in this program's own process group and at
the timeout subprocess.run ended its one process, the shell, by SIGKILL: what
the shell had started ran on, measured on 11 November 2026 at 46030ab on
macOS, and under Linux by the measurement that came with the order of that
day, a sleep 30 with parent 1 standing a second after the self-test had
passed. A step in a session of its own is reached by no signal sent to this
program's process group, Ctrl-C at a terminal and the SIGTERM of
bin/devloop-bounded among them, so that since the same day this program takes
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP in hand: ended by one of them, it ends the step
that runs the same way, removes its directory and exits with 128 and the
signal's number; a signal that was ignored when this program started stays
ignored, as SIGINT is where a shell without job control started it in the
background, and the step inherits that; ended by SIGKILL it leaves the step
running. A step that ends
in time and leaves a process behind is not touched, now as before: GitHub
ends such a process at the job's end, by the same variable. Under Windows
none of this is built and a step runs as before: at the timeout its one
process is ended and nothing it started, and the program then waits for the
step's output to close.

The file is read by this program's own reader of the YAML the workflows of
this repository are written in: mappings and sequences by indentation, a
sequence item opening a mapping on its own line, literal and folded block
scalars with their chomping, quoted and plain scalars, flow sequences in
brackets, comments outside block scalars; nothing else is needed for
.github/workflows/stock-take.yml, and a form it does not read ends with 2
and the line. It needs python3 and git, and ps where a step runs past the
timeout or the program is ended while a step runs, and no GNU tool. The
steps it runs need what they need on GitHub, python3 and git, and the self-test
needs bash, sh and, on macOS, Linux and Alpine, pgrep on PATH.

The self-test writes a workflow of its own and a throwaway repository with a
new entry head on a branch, runs this program over it, and reads the outcomes:
the default shell without pipefail against shell: bash with it on the same
block, set -e ending a block at the first failing command, a step's own shell
over the job's default, PR_TITLE out of the event, a block with a heredoc and
blank lines, the three kinds of step not run, a shell this program cannot
run, a job stopped at its timeout, a matrix job run once, --list running
nothing, exit 1 and 0 on a red and a green file, exit 2 on a file that is no
workflow; on macOS, Linux and Alpine, since 11 November 2026, the step past the
timeout ended with everything it started, a sleep that has lost its parent,
one in a process group of its own and the one its shell waits for, sleep 7361,
7363 and 7365, none of them found by pgrep -f once the program has returned,
under a pattern bound at the start of the command line, which a zombie, having
no command line, does not match, the program ended by SIGTERM while a step
runs, exit 143 and the step's sleeps 7367 and 7369 gone, and a step past the
timeout that runs this program one level down, the sleep 7371 of that one's
step gone as well, all three left out under Windows with a line that says so,
where the step past the timeout is the sleep 30 of before; at its end the
self-test sends SIGKILL to whatever pgrep still finds of those six sleeps, so
that a red case leaves none behind; the first line naming the system and its release, sysname and
release of os.uname(), or sys.platform where os.uname is missing, and no node
name, held against the text the self-test builds from the same two fields,
which it does not print where it is red, only the count of words in the
parenthesis and the count expected; and that the real workflow file reads and
carries the two names left out, so that the exclusion stays live. Nothing of
it reaches GitHub.
"""

import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

EXCLUDED = ("what the checkout holds", "what the container needs, under Alpine alone")
DEFAULT_WORKFLOW = ".github/workflows/stock-take.yml"
# the commands of the docs, {0} the file; "none" the row for a step without a shell on Linux and macOS
SHELLS = {
    "none": "bash -e {0}",
    "bash": "bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}",
    "sh": "sh -e {0}",
    "pwsh": "pwsh -command \". '{0}'\"",
    "python": "python {0}",
}
NOT_RUN_HERE = ("cmd", "powershell")
EXTENSION = {"pwsh": ".ps1", "powershell": ".ps1", "python": ".py", "cmd": ".cmd"}
TITLE_EXPR = re.compile(r"\$\{\{\s*github\.event\.pull_request\.title\s*\}\}")
ANY_EXPR = re.compile(r"\$\{\{.*?\}\}")


class YamlError(Exception):
    pass


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the reader

def read_yaml(text):
    """The subset of YAML the workflows here are written in, as nested dicts,
    lists and scalars."""
    lines = text.split("\n")
    pos = [0]

    def indent_of(s):
        return len(s) - len(s.lstrip(" "))

    def skip_blank(i):
        while i < len(lines) and (lines[i].strip() == "" or lines[i].lstrip().startswith("#")):
            i += 1
        return i

    def scalar(raw, lineno):
        s = raw.strip()
        if s.startswith('"'):
            m = re.match(r'"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"\s*(#.*)?$', s)
            if not m:
                raise YamlError("line %d: a double-quoted scalar does not close" % lineno)
            return bytes(m.group(1), "utf-8").decode("unicode_escape")
        if s.startswith("'"):
            m = re.match(r"'((?:[^']|'')*)'\s*(#.*)?$", s)
            if not m:
                raise YamlError("line %d: a single-quoted scalar does not close" % lineno)
            return m.group(1).replace("''", "'")
        cut = re.search(r"\s#", s)
        if cut:
            s = s[:cut.start()].rstrip()
        if s.startswith("[") and s.endswith("]"):
            inner = s[1:-1].strip()
            return [scalar(x, lineno) for x in inner.split(",")] if inner else []
        if s in ("true", "True"):
            return True
        if s in ("false", "False"):
            return False
        if s in ("null", "~", ""):
            return None
        if re.match(r"^-?[0-9]+$", s):
            return int(s)
        if re.match(r"^-?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$", s):
            return float(s)
        return s

    def block_scalar(header, key_indent, i):
        """A | or > block after a key: the lines indented deeper than the key,
        blank lines among them, to the first non-blank line that is not."""
        m = re.match(r"^([|>])([+-]?)\s*(#.*)?$", header.strip())
        if not m:
            raise YamlError("line %d: a block scalar header this reader does not know: %s" % (i, header.strip()))
        style, chomp = m.group(1), m.group(2)
        body = []
        block_indent = None
        j = i
        while j < len(lines):
            raw = lines[j]
            if raw.strip() == "":
                body.append("")
                j += 1
                continue
            ind = indent_of(raw)
            if ind <= key_indent:
                break
            if block_indent is None:
                block_indent = ind
            if ind < block_indent:
                raise YamlError("line %d: a line of a block scalar less indented than its first line" % (j + 1))
            body.append(raw[block_indent:])
            j += 1
        while body and body[-1] == "":
            body.pop()
        if style == "|":
            text = "\n".join(body)
        else:
            out, para = [], []
            for l in body:
                if l == "":
                    out.append(" ".join(para))
                    para = []
                else:
                    para.append(l)
            out.append(" ".join(para))
            text = "\n".join(out)
        if chomp == "-":
            return text, j
        if chomp == "+":
            return text + "\n" * (1 + (len(lines[i:j]) - len(body) if False else 1)), j
        return text + "\n", j

    def parse_block(i, indent):
        i = skip_blank(i)
        if i >= len(lines):
            return None, i
        if lines[i].lstrip().startswith("- ") or lines[i].strip() == "-":
            return parse_seq(i, indent_of(lines[i]))
        return parse_map(i, indent_of(lines[i]))

    def parse_map(i, indent, first=None):
        d = {}
        while True:
            i = skip_blank(i)
            if i >= len(lines):
                break
            raw = lines[i]
            ind = indent_of(raw)
            if ind < indent:
                break
            if ind > indent:
                raise YamlError("line %d: unexpected indentation" % (i + 1))
            if raw.lstrip().startswith("- "):
                break
            m = re.match(r"^\s*([A-Za-z0-9_.\-]+):(?:\s+(.*))?$", raw)
            if not m:
                raise YamlError("line %d: not a key of a mapping: %s" % (i + 1, raw.strip()))
            key, rest = m.group(1), (m.group(2) or "")
            if rest.strip().startswith("|") or rest.strip().startswith(">"):
                val, i = block_scalar(rest, ind, i + 1)
            elif rest.strip() == "" or rest.strip().startswith("#"):
                nxt = skip_blank(i + 1)
                if nxt < len(lines) and indent_of(lines[nxt]) > ind:
                    val, i = parse_block(nxt, indent_of(lines[nxt]))
                elif nxt < len(lines) and indent_of(lines[nxt]) == ind and lines[nxt].lstrip().startswith("- "):
                    val, i = parse_seq(nxt, ind)
                else:
                    val, i = None, i + 1
            else:
                val, i = scalar(rest, i + 1), i + 1
            if key in d:
                raise YamlError("line %d: the key %s stands twice in one mapping" % (i, key))
            d[key] = val
        return d, i

    def parse_seq(i, indent):
        items = []
        while True:
            i = skip_blank(i)
            if i >= len(lines):
                break
            raw = lines[i]
            ind = indent_of(raw)
            if ind != indent or not (raw.lstrip().startswith("- ") or raw.strip() == "-"):
                if ind > indent:
                    raise YamlError("line %d: unexpected indentation in a sequence" % (i + 1))
                break
            rest = raw.lstrip()[1:].lstrip()
            if rest == "" or rest.startswith("#"):
                val, i = parse_block(i + 1, 0)
                items.append(val)
                continue
            if re.match(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_.\-]+:(\s|$)", rest):
                item_indent = ind + (len(raw.lstrip()) - len(rest))
                # the first key stands on the item's line: parse the mapping as if the line were indented at item_indent
                lines[i] = " " * item_indent + rest
                val, i = parse_map(i, item_indent)
                items.append(val)
                continue
            items.append(scalar(rest, i + 1))
            i += 1
        return items, i

    value, i = parse_block(0, 0)
    i = skip_blank(i)
    if i < len(lines):
        raise YamlError("line %d: text after the document this reader could not place: %s" % (i + 1, lines[i].strip()))
    return value


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the plan

def this_system():
    s = sys.platform
    if s.startswith("win") or s == "cygwin" or s == "msys":
        return "Windows"
    if s == "darwin":
        return "macOS"
    return "Linux"


def shell_for(step, job, wf):
    for scope in (step, (job.get("defaults") or {}).get("run") or {}, (wf.get("defaults") or {}).get("run") or {}):
        if isinstance(scope, dict) and scope.get("shell"):
            return str(scope["shell"]), "the step" if scope is step else ("the job's defaults.run.shell" if scope is ((job.get("defaults") or {}).get("run") or {}) else "the workflow's defaults.run.shell")
    return None, "none given"


def command_for(shell, system):
    """The command GitHub's table gives, with {0}, and a reason where none runs here."""
    if shell is None:
        if system == "Windows":
            return None, "no shell given, and the default on Windows is pwsh, which this program does not run"
        if shutil.which("bash"):
            return SHELLS["none"], None
        return SHELLS["sh"], None
    if shell in NOT_RUN_HERE:
        return None, "the shell %s is not run here" % shell
    if shell in SHELLS:
        return SHELLS[shell], None
    if "{0}" in shell:
        return shell, None
    return None, "a shell this program does not know: %s" % shell


def working_dir(step, job, wf, root):
    d = None
    for scope in ((wf.get("defaults") or {}).get("run") or {}, (job.get("defaults") or {}).get("run") or {}, step):
        if isinstance(scope, dict) and scope.get("working-directory"):
            d = str(scope["working-directory"])
    return os.path.join(root, d) if d else root


def env_maps(step, job, wf):
    env = {}
    for scope in (wf, job, step):
        m = scope.get("env") if isinstance(scope, dict) else None
        if isinstance(m, dict):
            for k, v in m.items():
                env[str(k)] = "" if v is None else str(v)
    return env


def plan(wf, system):
    """One record per step: job id, step, name, shell, command or the reason it does not run."""
    jobs = wf.get("jobs")
    if not isinstance(jobs, dict) or not jobs:
        raise YamlError("the file has no jobs")
    out = []
    for job_id, job in jobs.items():
        if not isinstance(job, dict) or not isinstance(job.get("steps"), list):
            raise YamlError("the job %s has no steps" % job_id)
        matrix = ((job.get("strategy") or {}).get("matrix") or {}) if isinstance(job.get("strategy"), dict) else {}
        n_matrix = len(matrix.get("include") or []) if isinstance(matrix, dict) else 0
        timeout = job.get("timeout-minutes")
        for k, step in enumerate(job["steps"]):
            if not isinstance(step, dict):
                raise YamlError("step %d of the job %s is not a mapping" % (k + 1, job_id))
            name = str(step.get("name") or ("uses: " + str(step["uses"]) if step.get("uses") else (str(step.get("run") or "").strip().split("\n")[0] or "step %d" % (k + 1))))
            rec = {"job": job_id, "name": name, "matrix": n_matrix, "timeout": timeout, "run": step.get("run"), "step": step, "job_obj": job}
            if step.get("run") is None:
                rec["skip"] = "no run: block (uses: %s)" % step.get("uses") if step.get("uses") else "no run: block"
            elif name in EXCLUDED:
                rec["skip"] = "left out by the order of 31 October 2026"
            elif "if" in step:
                rec["red"] = "carries an if: field, %s, which this program does not evaluate" % step["if"]
            else:
                shell, source = shell_for(step, job, wf)
                cmd, why = command_for(shell, system)
                rec["shell"], rec["source"], rec["command"] = shell, source, cmd
                if why:
                    rec["red"] = why
            out.append(rec)
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the run

def entry_head(root):
    """The head of the new entry the branch adds to docs/roadmap.md, as the
    workflow's step on titles reads one; None where the diff adds none."""
    diff = subprocess.run(["git", "diff", "origin/main...HEAD", "--", "docs/roadmap.md"], cwd=root, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if diff.returncode != 0:
        return None, "git diff origin/main...HEAD did not answer: %s" % diff.stderr.strip()
    added = [l[1:] for l in diff.stdout.splitlines() if l.startswith("+- **")]
    if not added:
        return None, None
    try:
        lines = open(os.path.join(root, "docs/roadmap.md"), encoding="utf-8").read().split("\n")
    except OSError as e:
        return None, "docs/roadmap.md cannot be read: %s" % e
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.startswith("- **") and line in added:
            text = line[4:]
            j = i
            while "**" not in text and j + 1 < len(lines):
                j += 1
                text += " " + lines[j].strip()
            return text.split("**", 1)[0], None
    return None, "the added head is not in the file"


def run_step(rec, root, tmp, base_env, title):
    """Runs one step; returns (exit code or None, output, note)."""
    step, job, wf_env = rec["step"], rec["job_obj"], rec["wf_env"]
    env = dict(base_env)
    for k, v in env_maps(step, job, {"env": wf_env}).items():
        if TITLE_EXPR.search(v):
            v = TITLE_EXPR.sub(title or "", v)
        if ANY_EXPR.search(v):
            return None, "", "env %s carries an expression this program does not resolve: %s" % (k, v)
        env[k] = v
    text = str(rec["run"])
    if ANY_EXPR.search(text):
        return None, "", "the run: block carries an expression this program does not resolve: %s" % ANY_EXPR.search(text).group(0)
    ext = EXTENSION.get(rec["shell"] or "", "")
    fd, path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="step.", suffix=ext, dir=tmp)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(text if text.endswith("\n") else text + "\n")
    argv = [a.replace("{0}", path) for a in shlex.split(rec["command"])]
    cwd = working_dir(step, job, {"defaults": rec["wf_defaults"]}, root)
    timeout = rec["timeout"]
    try:
        rc, raw, stopped, left = run_bounded(argv, cwd, env, float(timeout) * 60 if timeout else None)
    except OSError as e:
        return None, "", "the shell could not be started: %s" % e
    out = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace") if raw else ""
    if stopped:
        return None, out, "stopped at the job's timeout of %s minutes, as GitHub stops the job" % timeout + ("; " + left if left else "")
    return rc, out, None


def listed():
    """Every process as (pid, parent, state), read off ps -A -o pid,ppid,stat,
    which the ps of macOS, of procps and of busybox take alike; None where ps
    is not there or does not answer so."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-A", "-o", "pid,ppid,stat"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None
    rows = []
    for line in r.stdout.decode("ascii", "replace").splitlines():
        f = line.split()
        if len(f) >= 2 and f[0].isdigit() and f[1].isdigit():
            rows.append((int(f[0]), int(f[1]), f[2] if len(f) > 2 else ""))
    return rows


def of_family(pid, family):
    """Whether the session or the process group of pid is led by a process of
    the family; False where the process is gone or may not be asked."""
    try:
        return os.getsid(pid) in family or os.getpgid(pid) in family
    except OSError:
        return False


def end_family(root):
    """Ends a step with everything it started, by SIGKILL: root is the step's
    shell, started as the leader of a session of its own. Listing after
    listing, every process whose parent, session or process group is a
    process already known as the step's, and with the first of them the
    step's process group at once, until a listing shows none that is not a
    zombie, five seconds at most. The first listing comes before the first
    signal: a process started one level down in a session of its own hangs on
    the step by its parent alone, and is tied to nothing once that parent is
    gone. Returns None where nothing of the step stands then, else a sentence
    saying what does."""
    family = {root}
    until = time.monotonic() + 5
    while True:
        rows = listed()
        try:
            os.killpg(root, signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            pass
        if rows is None:
            return "ps did not answer with pid, ppid and stat, so that the step's own process group alone was ended"
        grew = True
        while grew:
            grew = False
            for pid, parent, _ in rows:
                if pid not in family and (parent in family or of_family(pid, family)):
                    family.add(pid)
                    grew = True
        standing = [pid for pid, _, state in rows if pid in family and not state.startswith("Z")]
        if not standing:
            return None
        if time.monotonic() > until:
            return "%d processes it started still stood after five seconds of SIGKILL" % len(standing)
        for pid in standing:
            try:
                os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
        time.sleep(0.05)


def run_bounded(argv, cwd, env, limit):
    """Runs the command of a step, both of its streams read as one, limit
    seconds at most: (exit code, output, False, None) where it ended, and
    (None, the output so far, True, None or what could not be ended) where
    it was stopped at the limit. See "The end of a step" in the head."""
    if os.name != "posix":
        # Windows: as before 11 November 2026, the one process ended and nothing it started
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=cwd, env=env, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, timeout=limit)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
            return None, e.stdout or b"", True, None
        return p.returncode, p.stdout, False, None
    p = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=cwd, env=env, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True)
    try:
        raw, _ = p.communicate(timeout=limit)
        return p.returncode, raw, False, None
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        left = end_family(p.pid)
    except BaseException:
        # this program is ended itself, by a signal main() turns into an exit or by anything else: the step stands in a session of its own and would run on
        end_family(p.pid)
        p.wait()
        raise
    p.wait()
    try:
        raw, _ = p.communicate(timeout=5)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
        raw = e.stdout or b""
        left = left or "a process it started that this program did not find still holds its output"
    return None, raw, True, left


def ended(signum, frame):
    """This program ended by SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP: an exit with 128 and
    the signal's number, which run_bounded sees on its way out and ends the
    step that runs with."""
    raise SystemExit(128 + signum)


def main(argv):
    listing = False
    args = list(argv[1:])
    if args and args[0] == "--self-test":
        return selftest()
    if args and args[0] == "--list":
        listing = True
        args = args[1:]
    if len(args) > 1:
        sys.stderr.write("usage: devloop-run-workflow [--list] [WORKFLOW] | --self-test\n")
        return 2
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    root = os.environ.get("DEVLOOP_RUN_WORKFLOW_ROOT") or os.path.dirname(here)
    path = args[0] if args else os.path.join(root, DEFAULT_WORKFLOW)
    if not os.path.isabs(path):
        path = os.path.abspath(path)
    try:
        text = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read()
    except OSError as e:
        sys.stderr.write("the workflow file cannot be read: %s\n" % e)
        return 2
    try:
        wf = read_yaml(text)
        if not isinstance(wf, dict):
            raise YamlError("the file is no mapping at the top")
        steps = plan(wf, this_system())
    except YamlError as e:
        sys.stderr.write("not read as a workflow: %s\n" % e)
        return 2
    system = this_system()
    print("devloop-run-workflow: %s, run here on %s (%s); a job with a matrix runs once here" % (os.path.relpath(path, root) if path.startswith(root) else path, system, "%s %s" % (os.uname().sysname, os.uname().release) if hasattr(os, "uname") else sys.platform))
    title, why = entry_head(root)
    if why:
        print("PR_TITLE: not read: %s" % why)
    elif title is None:
        print("PR_TITLE: empty, git diff origin/main...HEAD adds no entry head to docs/roadmap.md")
    else:
        print("PR_TITLE: %s" % title)
    for rec in steps:
        rec["wf_env"] = wf.get("env") if isinstance(wf.get("env"), dict) else {}
        rec["wf_defaults"] = wf.get("defaults") if isinstance(wf.get("defaults"), dict) else {}
    if listing:
        for rec in steps:
            what = rec.get("skip") and "not run: " + rec["skip"] or rec.get("red") and "red: " + rec["red"] or "runs as %s (%s)" % (rec["command"], rec["source"])
            print("%s / %s: %s%s" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], what, "; a matrix of %d, run once here" % rec["matrix"] if rec["matrix"] else ""))
        print("listed, nothing run")
        return 0
    if os.name == "posix":
        for s in (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
            if signal.getsignal(s) != signal.SIG_IGN:
                signal.signal(s, ended)
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-run-workflow.")
    base_env = dict(os.environ)
    base_env.update({"GITHUB_WORKSPACE": root, "RUNNER_OS": system, "RUNNER_TEMP": tmp})
    green, red, skipped = 0, [], {}
    last_job = None
    try:
        for rec in steps:
            if rec["job"] != last_job:
                last_job = rec["job"]
                if rec["matrix"]:
                    print("== job %s: a matrix of %d systems on GitHub; here once, on %s" % (rec["job"], rec["matrix"], system))
            if rec.get("skip"):
                print("== %s / %s: not run, %s" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], rec["skip"]))
                skipped[rec["skip"]] = skipped.get(rec["skip"], 0) + 1
                continue
            if rec.get("red"):
                print("== %s / %s: not run, %s" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], rec["red"]))
                red.append("%s / %s: %s" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], rec["red"]))
                continue
            print("== %s / %s, as %s (%s)" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], rec["command"], rec["source"]))
            sys.stdout.flush()
            rc, out, note = run_step(rec, root, tmp, base_env, title)
            if out:
                sys.stdout.write(out if out.endswith("\n") else out + "\n")
            if note:
                print("-- not finished: %s" % note)
                red.append("%s / %s: %s" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], note))
            elif rc != 0:
                print("-- exit %d" % rc)
                red.append("%s / %s: exit %d" % (rec["job"], rec["name"], rc))
            else:
                print("-- exit 0")
                green += 1
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    by_design = "; ".join("%s: %d" % (k, v) for k, v in skipped.items()) or "none"
    if red:
        print("red: %d of %d steps run did not end with 0 or did not run here (%s); not run by design: %s" % (len(red), len(red) + green, "; ".join(red), by_design))
        return 1
    print("green: %d steps run, every one ended with 0; not run by design: %s" % (green, by_design))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- self-test

TEST_WORKFLOW = """# a workflow for the self-test of devloop-run-workflow
name: self-test

on:
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]

env:
  FROM_WORKFLOW: wf

jobs:
  plain:
    name: plain
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0

      - name: what the checkout holds
        run: |
          echo "never run here"
          exit 7

      - name: green
        run: echo green-ran

      # a comment between steps
      - name: a pipe without pipefail
        run: false | true

      - name: set -e stops the block
        run: |
          false
          echo after-false

      - name: a sh step
        shell: sh
        run: echo sh-ran

      - name: the title out of the event
        env:
          PR_TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}
          FROM_STEP: st
        run: |
          printf 'title=%s\\n' "$PR_TITLE"
          printf 'wf=%s step=%s\\n' "$FROM_WORKFLOW" "$FROM_STEP"
          printf 'os=%s ws=%s\\n' "$RUNNER_OS" "${GITHUB_WORKSPACE##*/}"

      - name: a heredoc with blank lines and a hash
        run: |
          python3 - <<'PY'
          x = 1

          # not a comment of the workflow
          print("heredoc", x + 1)
          PY

      - name: under a condition
        if: matrix.system == 'alpine'
        run: echo conditional

      - name: what the container needs, under Alpine alone
        if: matrix.system == 'alpine'
        shell: sh
        run: exit 9

  with-bash:
    name: with bash
    runs-on: ${{ matrix.runner }}
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        include:
          - system: one
            runner: ubuntu-latest
          - system: two
            runner: macos-latest
    timeout-minutes: TIMEOUT
    defaults:
      run:
        shell: bash
    steps:
      - name: a pipe with pipefail
        run: false | true

      - name: the step's shell over the job's
        shell: sh
        run: echo step-shell

      - name: a shell this program does not know
        shell: fish
        run: echo fish

      - name: an expression in the block
        run: echo ${{ matrix.system }}

      - name: past the timeout
        run: |
          STEP_PAST_IT
"""
# the block of the step past the timeout: an orphan in the step's own process group, a sleep in a group of its own, and the sleep the shell waits for; under Windows the one sleep of before
PAST_IT = ("( sleep 7361 & )", "set -m", "sleep 7363 &", "set +m", "echo three-started", "sleep 7365") if os.name == "posix" else ("sleep 30",)


def selftest():
    import json
    ok = [True]
    n = [0]

    def case(name, cond, detail=""):
        n[0] += 1
        if cond:
            print("ok   " + name)
        else:
            ok[0] = False
            print("FAIL " + name + (": " + detail if detail else ""))

    def found(pattern):
        """The process numbers pgrep -f finds for a pattern bound at the start
        of the command line, which a zombie, having none, does not match; None
        where pgrep is not on PATH or does not answer."""
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-f", pattern], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return None
        return [int(x) for x in r.stdout.split()] if r.returncode in (0, 1) else None

    posix = os.name == "posix"
    sleeps = r"^sleep 73(61|63|65|67|69|71)$"
    T = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-run-workflow-selftest.")
    try:
        # the reader
        y = read_yaml("a: 1\nb:\n  - x: 'q''q'\n    y: \"t\\n\"\n  - plain text # c\nc: |\n  l1\n\n  l2\nd: >-\n  f1\n  f2\n\n  f3\ne: [p, q]\nf: true\n")
        case("the reader: ints, quotes, a sequence of mappings, literal and folded blocks, a flow sequence, a boolean",
             y == {"a": 1, "b": [{"x": "q'q", "y": "t\n"}, "plain text"], "c": "l1\n\nl2\n", "d": "f1 f2\nf3", "e": ["p", "q"], "f": True}, json.dumps(y))
        for bad, word in (("a: |x\n  l\n", "block scalar header"), ("a:\n  b: 1\n c: 2\n", "indentation"), ("a: 1\na: 2\n", "twice")):
            try:
                read_yaml(bad)
                case("the reader refuses: " + word, False, "read without an error")
            except YamlError as e:
                case("the reader refuses: " + word, word in str(e), str(e))
        case("the shell table: none, bash, sh, custom, unknown, cmd",
             command_for(None, "Linux")[0] == "bash -e {0}" and command_for("bash", "Linux")[0] == SHELLS["bash"] and command_for("sh", "macOS")[0] == "sh -e {0}"
             and command_for("zsh -e {0}", "Linux")[0] == "zsh -e {0}" and command_for("fish", "Linux")[0] is None and command_for("cmd", "Windows")[0] is None
             and command_for(None, "Windows")[0] is None)
        # a throwaway repository with origin/main and a branch that adds an entry head
        repo = os.path.join(T, "repo")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(repo, "docs"))
        git = ["git", "-c", "user.name=devloop-selftest", "-c", "user.email=selftest@example.invalid"]
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", "-b", "main", repo], check=True)
        open(os.path.join(repo, "docs/roadmap.md"), "w").write("# r\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- **An old head.** text\n")
        subprocess.run(git + ["-C", repo, "add", "."], check=True)
        subprocess.run(git + ["-C", repo, "commit", "-q", "-m", "init"], check=True)
        subprocess.run(["git", "-C", repo, "update-ref", "refs/remotes/origin/main", "main"], check=True)
        subprocess.run(["git", "-C", repo, "checkout", "-q", "-b", "work"], check=True)
        open(os.path.join(repo, "docs/roadmap.md"), "a").write("- **A new head that\n  wraps.** more text\n")
        subprocess.run(git + ["-C", repo, "add", "."], check=True)
        subprocess.run(git + ["-C", repo, "commit", "-q", "-m", "entry"], check=True)
        case("the entry head: read to its closing ** across a line break", entry_head(repo) == ("A new head that wraps.", None), repr(entry_head(repo)))
        wf = os.path.join(T, "wf.yml")
        open(wf, "w").write(TEST_WORKFLOW.replace("TIMEOUT", "0.02").replace("STEP_PAST_IT", "\n          ".join(PAST_IT)))
        me = os.path.abspath(__file__)
        env = dict(os.environ, DEVLOOP_RUN_WORKFLOW_ROOT=repo)
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, wf], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        out = p.stdout
        case("a red file ends with 1 and a last line opening with red:", p.returncode == 1 and out.rstrip().split("\n")[-1].startswith("red: "), out[-400:])
        # the first line: the parenthesis holds sysname and release, or sys.platform, and no node name; where red, the line is not printed, only the counts of words
        first = out.split("\n")[0]
        paren = " ".join([os.uname().sysname, os.uname().release]) if hasattr(os, "uname") else sys.platform
        words = len(" ".join(re.findall(r" \((.*)\); a job with a matrix runs once here$", first)).split())
        case("the first line names the system and its release, or sys.platform, and no node name",
             first == "devloop-run-workflow: %s, run here on %s (%s); a job with a matrix runs once here" % (wf, this_system(), paren),
             "the parenthesis carries %d words, %d expected" % (words, len(paren.split())))
        case("PR_TITLE out of the event: the new head, printed first and reaching the step", "PR_TITLE: A new head that wraps." in out and "title=A new head that wraps." in out, out[:600])
        case("the workflow's env and the step's env reach the step, with RUNNER_OS and GITHUB_WORKSPACE", "wf=wf step=st" in out and ("os=%s ws=repo" % this_system()) in out, out)
        case("a step without a shell runs as bash -e {0}", "/ green, as bash -e {0} (none given)" in out and "green-ran" in out, out)
        case("without pipefail a failing pipe passes", "plain / a pipe without pipefail, as bash -e {0}" in out and out.split("plain / a pipe without pipefail")[1].split("== ")[0].strip().endswith("-- exit 0"), out)
        case("set -e ends the block at the first failing command", "after-false" not in out and "plain / set -e stops the block" in out and out.split("plain / set -e stops the block")[1].split("== ")[0].strip().endswith("-- exit 1"), out)
        case("a sh step runs as sh -e {0}", "/ a sh step, as sh -e {0} (the step)" in out and "sh-ran" in out, out)
        case("a heredoc with blank lines and a hash line reaches the shell whole", "heredoc 2" in out, out)
        case("uses: without run: not run", "uses: actions/checkout@v7: not run, no run: block (uses: actions/checkout@v7)" in out, out)
        case("the two names of the order left out, before and with an if:", "what the checkout holds: not run, left out by the order" in out and "under Alpine alone: not run, left out by the order" in out and "never run here" not in out, out)
        case("a step under an if: not run and red", "under a condition: not run, carries an if: field, matrix.system == 'alpine'" in out and "under a condition: carries an if:" in out.rstrip().split("\n")[-1], out)
        case("a matrix job said to run once here", "== job with-bash: a matrix of 2 systems on GitHub; here once" in out, out)
        case("the job's defaults.run.shell: with pipefail the same pipe fails", "with-bash / a pipe with pipefail, as bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0} (the job's defaults.run.shell)" in out and out.split("with-bash / a pipe with pipefail")[1].split("== ")[0].strip().endswith("-- exit 1"), out)
        case("the step's shell over the job's default", "/ the step's shell over the job's, as sh -e {0} (the step)" in out and "step-shell" in out, out)
        case("a shell this program does not know: red, said", "a shell this program does not know: fish" in out, out)
        case("an expression in the block: not run, red", "the run: block carries an expression this program does not resolve: ${{ matrix.system }}" in out, out)
        case("a step past the job's timeout: stopped, red", "stopped at the job's timeout of 0.02 minutes" in out, out)
        if posix:
            left = found(r"^sleep 73(61|63|65)$")
            case("a step past the job's timeout: ended with everything it started, a sleep without a parent, one in a process group of its own and the one its shell waits for, none found by pgrep once the program has returned",
                 "three-started" in out and left == [] and "as GitHub stops the job;" not in out,
                 "pgrep is not on PATH or did not answer" if left is None else "started: %s; still found: %s" % ("three-started" in out, left))
        else:
            print("left out under Windows: that a step past the job's timeout is ended with everything it started, since this program ends there the step's one process and nothing it started, as before; its step past the timeout is a sleep of thirty seconds there")
        last = out.rstrip().split("\n")[-1]
        case("the last line counts the red steps and those not run by design", last.startswith("red: 6 of 12 steps run did not end with 0 or did not run here (") and "not run by design: no run: block (uses: actions/checkout@v7): 1; left out by the order of 31 October 2026: 2" in last, last)
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "--list", wf], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        case("--list prints the plan and runs nothing", p.returncode == 0 and p.stdout.rstrip().endswith("listed, nothing run") and "green-ran" not in p.stdout and "plain / green: runs as bash -e {0} (none given)" in p.stdout, p.stdout[-300:])
        green_wf = os.path.join(T, "green.yml")
        open(green_wf, "w").write("jobs:\n  g:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: one\n        run: echo one\n      - name: two\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          echo two\n")
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, green_wf], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        case("a green file: exit 0, the last line green: 2 steps run", p.returncode == 0 and p.stdout.rstrip().split("\n")[-1] == "green: 2 steps run, every one ended with 0; not run by design: none", p.stdout[-300:])
        open(os.path.join(T, "none.yml"), "w").write("name: x\n")
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, os.path.join(T, "none.yml")], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        case("a file without jobs: exit 2, not read as a workflow", p.returncode == 2 and "not read as a workflow: the file has no jobs" in p.stderr, p.stderr)
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, os.path.join(T, "missing.yml")], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        case("a missing file: exit 2", p.returncode == 2 and "cannot be read" in p.stderr, p.stderr)
        p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, "a", "b"], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        case("two arguments: usage, exit 2", p.returncode == 2 and p.stderr.startswith("usage:"), p.stderr)
        # ended itself while a step runs: the step, in a session of its own, ended with everything it started
        if posix:
            term_wf = os.path.join(T, "term.yml")
            open(term_wf, "w").write("jobs:\n  g:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: runs on\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          set -m\n          sleep 7367 &\n          set +m\n          sleep 7369\n")
            q = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, me, term_wf], env=env, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT)
            until = time.monotonic() + 30
            while time.monotonic() < until and len(found(r"^sleep 73(67|69)$") or []) < 2:
                time.sleep(0.1)
            started = found(r"^sleep 73(67|69)$")
            q.send_signal(signal.SIGTERM)
            try:
                q.communicate(timeout=60)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                end_family(q.pid)
                q.communicate()
            left = found(r"^sleep 73(67|69)$")
            case("ended by SIGTERM while a step runs: the step ended with everything it started, exit 143",
                 started is not None and len(started) == 2 and q.returncode == 143 and left == [],
                 "pgrep is not on PATH or did not answer" if started is None or left is None else "found before: %s; exit %s; still found: %s" % (started, q.returncode, left))
            # a step past the timeout that runs this program one level down: the step of that one stands in a session of its own and hangs on the outer step by its parent alone
            inner_wf, nest_wf = os.path.join(T, "inner.yml"), os.path.join(T, "nest.yml")
            open(inner_wf, "w").write("jobs:\n  g:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: one level down\n        shell: bash\n        run: sleep 7371\n")
            open(nest_wf, "w").write("jobs:\n  g:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 0.05\n    steps:\n      - name: this program one level down\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          \"$SELFTEST_PY\" \"$SELFTEST_ME\" \"$SELFTEST_INNER\"\n")
            p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me, nest_wf], env=dict(env, SELFTEST_PY=sys.executable, SELFTEST_ME=me, SELFTEST_INNER=inner_wf), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
            left = found(r"^sleep 7371$")
            case("a step past the job's timeout that runs this program one level down: the step of that one, in a session of its own, ended too",
                 p.returncode == 1 and "stopped at the job's timeout of 0.05 minutes" in p.stdout and "g / one level down, as bash" in p.stdout and left == [],
                 "pgrep is not on PATH or did not answer" if left is None else "exit %s; the step one level down announced: %s; still found: %s" % (p.returncode, "g / one level down, as bash" in p.stdout, left))
        else:
            print("left out under Windows: that this program, ended by a signal while a step runs, ends the step, and that a step past the timeout which runs this program one level down is ended with the step of that one, since it starts a step in no session of its own there and takes no signal in hand")
        # the real workflow reads, and the exclusion stays live
        real = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(me)), DEFAULT_WORKFLOW)
        try:
            real_wf = read_yaml(open(real, encoding="utf-8").read())
            steps = plan(real_wf, this_system())
            names = [r["name"] for r in steps]
            case("the real workflow reads: jobs stock-take and hooks-on-every-system, every step with a name or uses", list(real_wf["jobs"]) == ["stock-take", "hooks-on-every-system"] and all(names), repr(names))
            case("the two names the order left out stand in the real workflow", all(any(nm == e for nm in names) for e in EXCLUDED), repr(names))
            case("in the real workflow the job stock-take runs as bash -e {0} and hooks on every system as bash with pipefail",
                 all(r.get("command") == "bash -e {0}" for r in steps if r["job"] == "stock-take" and r.get("command")) and all(r.get("command") == SHELLS["bash"] for r in steps if r["job"] == "hooks-on-every-system" and r.get("command")), repr([(r["job"], r.get("command")) for r in steps]))
            case("the real workflow's title step carries the event's title in its env", any(TITLE_EXPR.search(str(((r["step"].get("env") or {}).get("PR_TITLE") or ""))) for r in steps), "")
        except (OSError, YamlError) as e:
            case("the real workflow reads", False, str(e))
    finally:
        # nothing of the self-test's own steps runs on, whatever case was red or raised
        for pid in (found(sleeps) or []) if posix else []:
            try:
                os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)
    if ok[0]:
        print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
        return 0
    print("SELF-TEST FAILED")
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
